voltage regulator output is shorted to the UART3 connector lines, which can damage the internal regulator or the connector drivers and leaves UART3 non-functional.\", \"confidence\": 0.45}, {\"category\": \"wrong_value\", \"components\": [\"C59\"], \"pins\": [\"C59.1\", \"C59.2\"], \"nets\": [\"Net-(U8-C1)\"], \"observed\": \"C59 is 220n and sits on the magnetometer C1 pin (U8 pin 5).\", \"expected\": \"The IIS2MDCTR datasheet requires a 100 nF ceramic on the C1 pin, so 220n is a deviation from the part's recommended value.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(comp (ref \\\"C59\\\") (value \\\"220n\\\")\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U8\\\") (pin \\\"5\\\") (pinfunction \\\"C1\\\") (pintype \\\"bidirectional\\\"))\"}], \"impact\": \"The magnetometer's internal regulator decoupling is off-spec, which may cause small changes in noise or stability. The effect is likely minor.\", \"confidence\": 0.2}], \"summary\": \"The most likely anomaly is the PCA9685 A0 pin tied high, which moves the driver to address 0x41 instead of the specified 0x40. The UART3 nets also land on the H7's VCAP pins, which would be a short if real.\"}",
